{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Buffalo, Rochester and Pittsburgh Railway (BR&P), the major north-south railroad through Punxsutawney, PA and a key coal-hauling line across the Allegheny Plateau. Built as the Rochester and Pittsburgh Railroad (with a separate Buffalo-Ashford, NY segment also completed 1883), it reached Punxsutawney on Sept 15 1883 via Bradford, Ridgway, and DuBois, PA -- DuBois became the site of a major BR&P locomotive and car repair facility, and Punxsutawney offered interchange with the Pennsylvania Railroad's Allegheny Valley line. The railroad extended south through Indiana, PA to Pittsburgh. It defaulted and fell into bankruptcy in 1884, and was reorganized as the Buffalo, Rochester & Pittsburgh Railway when financier Adrian Iselin bought it Oct 16 1885. The Baltimore and Ohio Railroad took over BR&P operations in January 1932 (acquiring it alongside the neighboring Buffalo and Susquehanna). The last passenger train ran over the former BR&P trackage Oct 15 1955, when the B&O ended its Buffalo-Pittsburgh service. GEOMETRY UPGRADED 2026-08-24: replaced the original 9-point hand-placed waypoint approximation with real-traced OpenStreetMap track geometry (Overpass API, shortest-path graph search) -- most of this corridor is still active today as Norfolk Southern/CSX freight trackage through the Allegheny Plateau. Every hop except Buffalo-Ashford resolved to a connected, plausible real track path. The Buffalo-Ashford stretch (~53km) is a straight-line approximation instead: the real active track between those two points loops out through the Salamanca, NY area before doubling back to Ashford (a real but redundant detour, given Salamanca is the next waypoint after Ashford anyway), so a straight line was used rather than that implausible path -- flagged honestly rather than presented as accurate track.", "sources": ["https://journals.psu.edu/wph/article/download/3119/2950/ (Brockwayville and Punxsutawney Railroad construction began 1882, completed and turned over to the Rochester and Pittsburgh Railroad Sept 15 1883; route Salamanca-Bradford-Ridgway-DuBois-Punxsutawney; interchange with Allegheny Valley Railroad/PRR at Punxsutawney and NYC at DuBois; bankruptcy after defaulting summer 1884; Iselin purchased it Oct 16 1885 via the Pittsburgh & State Line Railroad (PA) and Buffalo, Rochester & Pittsburgh Railroad (NY))", "https://nyrails.org/places/west-valley-ny/ (Buffalo-Ashford, NY segment also built 1883)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buffalo,_Rochester_and_Pittsburgh_Railway (Baltimore and Ohio took over BR&P operations January 1932, along with the Buffalo and Susquehanna; last passenger train Oct 15 1955 when B&O ended Buffalo-Pittsburgh service)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indiana_station_(Pennsylvania) (BR&P's Indiana, PA passenger station, confirming the line's southern routing through Indiana en route to Pittsburgh)"], "not_yet_sourced": "Exact date the line was extended south from Punxsutawney through Indiana, PA to Pittsburgh (only the 1883 Punxsutawney milestone and the line's eventual full extent are sourced); precise frequency for either era, all estimated.", "geometry_sources": ["OpenStreetMap (via Overpass API, overpass.openstreetmap.fr mirror): railway=rail (active) combined with railway=abandoned/disused tagging, Buffalo NY to Pittsburgh PA via Ashford, Salamanca, Bradford, Ridgway, DuBois, Punxsutawney, and Indiana; routed via Dijkstra shortest path between each station's geocoded coordinates. All hops resolved to connected, plausible paths except Buffalo-Ashford, which triggered the implausible-detour check (156.7km real vs 52.7km straight) and was replaced with a straight-line fallback."], "pulled": "20260824", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Rochester and Pittsburgh Railroad / Buffalo, Rochester and Pittsburgh Railway", "line": "Rochester and Pittsburgh: BR&P", "id": "brp-buffalo-punxsutawney-pittsburgh", "color": "#5B4B8A", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 35, "speed_source": "estimated: late 19th/early 20th-century steam-era coal-hauling railroad with frequent stops; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1883, "end": 1932, "headway_min": 480, "note": "Rochester and Pittsburgh Railroad completed the line to Punxsutawney Sept 15 1883 via Bradford, Ridgway, and DuBois (with a separate Buffalo-Ashford segment also built 1883); defaulted into bankruptcy 1884, reorganized as the Buffalo, Rochester and Pittsburgh Railway when Adrian Iselin bought it Oct 16 1885; extended south through Indiana, PA to Pittsburgh; frequency estimated/unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://journals.psu.edu/wph/article/download/3119/2950/", "https://nyrails.org/places/west-valley-ny/", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buffalo", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indiana_station_(Pennsylvania"]}]}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-78.87815, 42.88642], [-78.65339, 42.44197], [-78.65378, 42.44994], [-78.65208, 42.44643], [-78.64948, 42.4435], [-78.64418, 42.43972], [-78.63891, 42.43754], [-78.63356, 42.43621], [-78.6315, 42.43509], [-78.63332, 42.43691], [-78.63239, 42.43567], [-78.62841, 42.43302], [-78.62652, 42.43281], [-78.62167, 42.4338], [-78.61996, 42.43351], [-78.61885, 42.43288